  "title": "Kourovka problem 21.3 (main)",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "pdf-text-entry",
    "text": "Let G = An or Sn and let H, K be soluble subgroups of G. For all sufficiently\nlarge n, can we always find an element x ∈ G such that H ∩ K x = 1? Does this hold\nfor all n ⩾ 21?\n    Note that the conclusion is false when G = S20 and H = K = (S4 ≀ S4 ) × S4 .\n                                         M. Anagnostopoulou-Merkouri, T. C. Burness",
